ation originated partly in Great Society social welfare initiatives that linked assumptions about African Americans and urban disorder to expanded policing and punishment.","confidence":0.96},"academicOrientation":{"score":68,"confidence":0.91},"profileConfidence":0.95,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"1b918a0636b1a4dd10a94aa7db644cb29316daffeac2742235ff4a7519855edc","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"True Crime & Justice","secondarySubjects":["Politics & Government","Race & Ethnicity"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily examines the impact of political decisions on mass incarceration, making it a significant work within True Crime & Justice, while also addressing important aspects of politics and race.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"0f87aa089e55d61cfda57143b5f6a585037af443253ef4fe55160762b51e38db","summary":"\"In the United States today, one in every 31 adults is under some form of penal control, including one in eleven African American men. How did the \"land of the free\" become the home of the world's largest prison system? Challenging the belief that America's prison problem originated with the Reagan administration's War on Drugs, Elizabeth Hinton traces the rise of mass incarceration to an ironic source: the social welfare programs of Lyndon Johnson's Great Society at the height of the civil rights era. Johnson's War on Poverty policies sought to foster equality and economic opportunity. But these initiatives were also rooted in widely shared assumptions about African Americans' role in urban disorder, which prompted Johnson to call for a simultaneous War on Crime. The 1965 Law Enforcement Assistance Act empowered the national government to take a direct role in militarizing local police. Federal anticrime funding soon incentivized social service providers to ally with police departments, courts, and prisons. Under Richard Nixon and his successors, welfare programs fell by the wayside while investment in policing and punishment expanded. Anticipating future crime, policy makers urged states to build new prisons and introduced law enforcement measures into urban schools and public housing, turning neighborhoods into targets of police surveillance.","publicationYear":2016,"displaySummary":"The 1965 Law Enforcement Assistance Act empowered the national government to take a direct role in militarizing local police. Federal anticrime funding soon incentivized social service providers to ally with police departments, courts, and prisons. Under Richard Nixon and his successors, welfare programs fell by the wayside while investment in policing and punishment expanded. Anticipating future crime, policy makers urged states to build new prisons and introduced law enforcement measures into urban schools and public housing, turning neighborhoods into targets of police surveillance.","primaryTopic":"Civil Rights & Racial Justice","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"True Crime & Justice","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"True Crime & Justice","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"True Crime & Justice","mappedSubject":"True Crime & Justice","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily examines the impact of political decisions on mass incarceration, making it a significant work within True Crime & Justice, while also addressing important aspects of politics and race.","id":"subject-evidence-book-from-the-war-on-poverty-to-the-war-on-crime-the-making-of-mass-incarceration-in-america-elizabet-1"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"SOCIAL SCIENCE","mappedSubject":"Society & Culture","score":5,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-from-the-war-on-poverty-to-the-war-on-crime-the-making-of-mass-incarceration-in-america-elizabet","id":"subject-evidence-book-from-the-war-on-pove